You'll find that, for game code, Nethack is beautiful. Games usually 
contain a few things that would make you ComSci teacher shudder. Chief 
among these:

a) Globals. There are a lot of them. They may make the structure more 
complicated, and bring in complexity if there are more than a few 
programmers, but they save overhead, and they writing a game without 
them (I've tried) is nearly impossible.

b) Macros. Preprocessor stuff. There not as bad as some would claim. 
They make it harder for a budding programmer, but in the case of Nethack 
they make porting a lot easier.

c) Bloody long lists of static things. My professors always told me to 
load large stuff from seperate files (*.dat files in a Windows 
environment), but I don't mind. Again, avoiding the extra file i/o at 
the beginning of a game can be valuable, especially if you run Nethack 
on an older system.

If you want to learn C, there is better source to look at, but it helps 
to read kluged "real world" code and understand why the programmers did 
the things they did.

